1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,151
A gecko can hold onto a wall.

2
00:00:02,151 --> 00:00:04,969
So how does it let go
with every step?

3
00:00:04,969 --> 00:00:07,639
The secret isn't just a strong grip.

4
00:00:07,639 --> 00:00:10,086
It's how that grip is controlled.

5
00:00:10,086 --> 00:00:15,351
Under a Tokay gecko's toes are tiny,
hair-like structures called setae.

6
00:00:15,351 --> 00:00:20,320
Their branching tips make extremely
close contact with the surface.

7
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,144
They aren't suction cups.

8
00:00:22,144 --> 00:00:26,813
At that tiny scale, van der Waals
forces help those tips adhere.

9
00:00:26,813 --> 00:00:28,418
But direction matters.

10
00:00:28,418 --> 00:00:34,036
A pull toward the base of the toe,
along the surface, helps support the grip.

11
00:00:34,036 --> 00:00:38,414
To release, a gecko can curl
its toes away from the surface.

12
00:00:38,414 --> 00:00:41,040
This movement helps the foot detach.

13
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:44,445
That toe movement is called
digital hyperextension.

14
00:00:44,445 --> 00:00:49,051
Don't confuse it with the angles
of the microscopic hairs beneath it.

15
00:00:49,051 --> 00:00:52,589
So a gecko doesn't simply
yank its foot off the wall.

16
00:00:52,589 --> 00:00:57,128
Close contact, the direction of loading,
and controlled toe movement

17
00:00:57,128 --> 00:01:00,800
work together to make gripping
and letting go possible.
